//! The extent chain: how a slab's capacity stops being a compile-time fact.
//!
//! A slab is a `Slab<T, N>`. The inline static array is extent 0, so the kernel
//! boots with the same compiled floor and before any donation exists; a
//! `SlabDonate` appends a donated extent, and the capacity that allocators and
//! teardown walks read is the sum.
//!
//! The spelling at every use site is the plain array's. `EVENTS[i].state` works
//! because `[]` is a `borrows` accessor that lends the slot where it sits:
//! inside the inline array for an index below the floor, inside donated memory
//! above it, so `&var EVENTS[i]` still borrows a real slot. Loop bounds are
//! different: an allocator scanning `0..MAX_EVENTS` would never see a donated
//! slot, so every loop reads `capacity()`.
//!
//! # The one access funnel
//!
//! `extent_addr` below is the only place a donated extent's memory becomes an
//! address. Every dereference of a donated slot goes through it, and it
//! converts through `hal.phys_to_virt`.
//!
//! # Why a generic
//!
//! Every slab kind wants the same three things (an inline extent 0, a chain of
//! donated ones, an index-to-place lookup); only the slot type and the floor
//! differ. A const-generic parameter carries the floor into the type
//! (`Slab<EventSlot, MAX_EVENTS>` is a distinct type from
//! `Slab<TimerSlot, MAX_TIMERS>`), so there is one body to review.
//!
//! # Kinds with parallel storage
//!
//! A kind with storage parallel to the slot array, keyed by the same index,
//! must not grow the slots and leave the storage behind. Two shapes are
//! admitted:
//!
//! - Fold the satellites into the slot. Pipe exchanges are fields of one
//!   `Exchange` record, so the exchanges are an ordinary `Slab` of their own.
//!   It grows in lockstep with the connection slab because the flat exchange
//!   index (`x = c * PIPE_INFLIGHT + i`) is uniform across the chain, which
//!   makes the two chains' extent boundaries agree by arithmetic. See
//!   `kcore.objects`' exchange-record header for the proof.
//!
//! - Add a column to the extent table. A thread's trap frame must be aligned
//!   (`THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE`), and no struct layout gives that on every target,
//!   so it lives in a side arena. `ext_side` is read through the same `locate`
//!   walk as the slot lookup, so slot `i`'s frame and storage cannot disagree
//!   about which extent they are in.
//!
//! A satellite must become part of the slot or a chain the same `locate`
//! answers for. A second table is not allowed.

// The linear-map seam for the access funnel below; the identity where the
// kernel runs at physical addresses.
import hal

import kcore.limits.{MAX_SLAB_CAPACITY, MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS}

/// A slab: an inline extent 0 of `N` slots, plus up to `MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS`
/// donated extents.
///
/// Extent 0 is the static array, which is what lets the kernel boot with no
/// donation: `total` starts at `N`, and every allocator, teardown sweep and
/// census reads `total`.
///
/// The extent table is a set of parallel fixed arrays rather than a linked
/// list, because a chain of at most `MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS` entries walked from the
/// front is simpler, and there is no allocator underneath a slab to allocate
/// nodes from.
///
/// `total` is cached rather than summed on demand: `capacity()` is read once
/// per loop iteration in several walks, and a sum over the extent table at each
/// would cost measurable time.
public(package) struct Slab<T, const N: Int> {
    /// Extent 0 — the compiled floor, and the whole slab until something
    /// donates.
    public(package) inline: [T; N],
    /// Each donated extent's base address. Physical, and it stays physical: a
    /// base is address-as-data, and only `extent_addr` converts.
    public(package) ext_base: [UInt; MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS],
    /// How many slots each donated extent holds.
    public(package) ext_slots: [Int; MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS],
    /// The side arena's base for this extent, physical on `ext_base`'s terms,
    /// and `0` for a kind that has no side arena.
    ///
    /// A side arena is per-slot storage that is not part of the slot. The
    /// thread frame arena is the one user: a saved context must be
    /// `THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE`-aligned and so cannot be a field of `ThreadSlot`
    /// (see `kcore.threads.frame_slot`). Every donated extent carries both
    /// arrays, and the two must be indexed by one boundary or a donated thread
    /// could read another thread's frame.
    ///
    /// It is a column of the extent table rather than a table of its own:
    /// `locate` answers `(extent, offset)` once, and both the slot address and
    /// the side-arena address are derived from that one answer, so there is no
    /// second lookup that could disagree with the first.
    ///
    /// Kinds with no side arena pay `MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS` words of `.bss` for a
    /// column they never read, and no image bytes.
    public(package) ext_side: [UInt; MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS],
    /// How many donated extents are live. Only grows: donation is permanent.
    public(package) ext_count: Int,
    /// `N` plus every donated extent's slots. The number every walk reads.
    public(package) total: Int,
}

extension Slab<T, N> {
    /// The one access funnel: the address of slot `i`, which must be at or
    /// above the floor.
    ///
    /// `ext_base[e]` stays physical, because a donated extent's base comes from
    /// a `Memory` capability and is address-as-data; only the moment of kernel
    /// dereference converts. This return is that moment, so the conversion is
    /// here and nowhere else. The conversion folds away on targets where the
    /// kernel runs at physical addresses. Nothing else in this module
    /// dereferences a donated extent; a missed conversion would be a hard fault
    /// on a translating target and invisible on the others.
    ///
    /// The walk is bounded by `MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS` and its body is O(1), so it
    /// takes no preemption point and needs none.
    public(package) func extent_addr(&self, i: Int) unsafe -> UInt {
        let (e, rest) = self.locate(i)
        hal.phys_to_virt(self.ext_base[e] + ((rest * sizeof<T>()) as UInt))
    }

    /// The one boundary walk: which donated extent holds slot `i`, and how far
    /// into it, for an `i` at or above the floor.
    ///
    /// A side arena (see `ext_side`) has to land in the same extent at the same
    /// offset as the slot it belongs to, so one walk answers `(extent, offset)`
    /// and both addresses are pure arithmetic on that pair.
    ///
    /// Bounded by `MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS` over an O(1) body, so it takes no
    /// preemption point, including on the context-switch path that reaches it
    /// via `frame_slot`.
    public(package) func locate(&self, i: Int) -> (Int, Int) {
        var rest = i - N
        var e = 0
        while e < self.ext_count {
            let here = self.ext_slots[e]
            if rest < here {
                return (e, rest)
            }
            rest = rest - here
            e = e + 1
        }
        panic("Slab.locate: index out of range: {} (len {})", i, self.total)
    }

    /// Extent `e`'s side arena base, physical exactly as `ext_base` is, and
    /// converted by whoever dereferences it (for threads,
    /// `kcore.threads.frame_slot`).
    public(package) func side_base(&self, e: Int) -> UInt { self.ext_side[e] }

    /// Slot `i`, lent where it sits.
    ///
    /// Declared `&var` with no shared twin, so every use borrows the slab
    /// exclusively, a read included: the root must be mutable, and two windows
    /// on one slab cannot be open at once. Every slab is a `var` static and no
    /// kernel code needs two; `@synthesize(shared)` would add shared reads.
    ///
    /// Out of range PANICS, in the house wording, and for the reason a direct
    /// accessor always does: an index past the capacity is a kernel bug, not a
    /// caller's mistake, because every index reaching here came from a slab
    /// scan or from a handle entry the table validated.
    public(package) func [](&var self, i: Int) unsafe borrows -> &var T {
        if i < 0 || i >= self.total {
            panic("Slab.[]: index out of range: {} (len {})", i, self.total)
        }
        if i < N {
            lend self.inline[i]
        } else {
            let p = self.extent_addr(i) as UnsafePointer<T>
            lend p[0]
        }
    }

    /// How many slots this slab holds right now: the compiled floor plus every
    /// donation. Allocators scan to this and teardown sweeps walk to it.
    public(package) func capacity(&self) -> Int { self.total }

    /// The compiled floor, extent 0's length. Used where a number has to be the
    /// one the image was built with rather than the one the deployment made.
    public(package) func floor(&self) -> Int { N }

    /// How many bytes one slot needs, which turns a donated region's length
    /// into a slot count.
    public(package) func slot_bytes(&self) -> Int { sizeof<T>() }

    /// What a donated extent's base address must be aligned to.
    public(package) func slot_align(&self) -> Int { alignof<T>() }

    /// How many more extents this slab can accept.
    public(package) func extents_left(&self) -> Int {
        MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS - self.ext_count
    }

    /// Append a donated extent of `slots` slots based at `base`.
    ///
    /// The caller has already validated the region, checked the alignment,
    /// worked out the slot count and zeroed the bytes; this only records.
    /// Answers `false` when the extent table is full or the donation would take
    /// the slab past `MAX_SLAB_CAPACITY`, which the caller turns into
    /// `NoResource`.
    ///
    /// The capacity test is in `donate_paired`, which every donation goes
    /// through and which is the one function that knows the running total. It
    /// is the runtime half of `MAX_SLAB_CAPACITY`'s asserts, which pin the
    /// constant against the handle table and against `Int` overflow; this keeps
    /// a real chain under it.
    public(package) func donate(&var self, base: UInt, slots: Int) -> Bool {
        self.donate_paired(base, slots, 0)
    }

    /// `donate` for a kind that carries a side arena: the same extent, plus the
    /// arena's base recorded in the same row.
    ///
    /// One function writes both columns, so an extent either exists with its
    /// arena or does not exist, and there is no ordering in which a slot is
    /// reachable and its frame is not. `donate` is this with `side = 0`.
    public(package) func donate_paired(&var self, base: UInt, slots: Int,
                                       side: UInt) -> Bool {
        if slots <= 0 { return false }
        if self.ext_count >= MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS { return false }
        if slots > MAX_SLAB_CAPACITY - self.total { return false }
        self.ext_base[self.ext_count] = base
        self.ext_slots[self.ext_count] = slots
        self.ext_side[self.ext_count] = side
        self.ext_count = self.ext_count + 1
        self.total = self.total + slots
        true
    }
}
